  "text_md": "PERSPECTIVE. A reflecting-glass. PERSPICIL. An optic-glass. It occurs in Albumazar, 1634, sig. B. iv. PERSTAND. To understand. Peek.\n\nMS. Morte Arthure, f. 89. PESATE. Is when a managed horse rises handsomely before and upon his haunches, and ax the same time bends his fore-legs up tohisbody. PESE. (1) Peace. Perceval 980, 981. (2) To sooth ; to appease.\n\nPERSTAND. To understand. Peele.\n\nPERSUADE . Persuasion.\n\nPERSUADERS. Spurs . pistols. Also;\n\nPERS WAY. To mitigate. Ben Jonson, iv. 428. PERT. Beautifully  ` delicate: It is the translation of subtilis in Gesta Rom. p. 142.\n\nTylle y be sewre of youre hartys ese,\n\nFor hete her clothes down sche dede\n\nMS. Cantab. Ff. i. G. f. 132. PESEN. Peas. This is the common early form of the word, and occurs in Chaucer, Legende of Good Women, 648. Holloway gives the following couplet, as seen lately on a board in a pea-field in Berkshire Shut the gate after you, I'll tell you the reason.\n\nAlmest to her gerdyl stede,\n\nThan lay sche uncovert ;\n\nSche was whyt as lylye yn May,\n\nOr snow that sneweth yn wynterys day,\n\nHe seygh never non sO pert.\n\nIllustrations of Fairy Mythology , P. 1l.\n\nBecause the pigs shouldn't get into the peason. Ben made\n\nPERTE. (1) To part. Still in use. Then Thomas a sory man was he. The terys ran out of his een gray Lufly lady, jet tell thou me If we shalle perte for ever and ay. MS. Cantab. Ff. v.\n\nin his 133d epigram.\n\nAs for his sallets, better never was\n\nthree-leav'd grasse, And for a sawee he seldome is at charges. For every crab-tree doth aflfbord him vergis ; His banket sometimes is greene beanes and peason. Nuts, peares, plumbes, apples, as they are in season. Taijor's Workes, 1630, i. 97-\n\n48, (2) Of good appearance. Ther was no man in the kynges lande More perte then was he. MS. Cantab. Ff. ii. 38,\n\nPERTELICHE. Openly. A-N)\n\nPESIBLE. Peaceable. {A.-N.) PESIBLETE. A calm. {A.-N.) PESK. A peach. Nominale MS.\n\nPresez hls penowne, and pertly ỉt hentes.\n\nPESK. A peach. Nominale MS-\n\nMorte Arthure , MS. Lincoln , f.84. PERTELOTE. The name of a hen.\n\nPESON An instrument in the form of a staff; with balls or crockets, used for weighing before scales were employed.\n\nPERTENERE. A partner.\n\nGod graunt u8 mekenesse in angurs here, And grace to lede owre lyfe here 800,\n\nPESS. A hassock. Suffolk.\n\nkind of merrymaking in summer evenings ; the treat, green field peas boiled in the shells. Yorksh. PESSIPE. A kind of cup.\n\nThat may aftur be pertenere\n\nOf hevene, whan we hens schall goo.\n\nPESSIPE. A kind of cup.\n\n# PERTRYCHE. A partridge.\n\nPESTERED. Crowded. Peele, ii. 235.\n\nPESTERMENT . Embarrassment. North.\n\nconstreyned undir the claues and nayles of the hauke, is as halfe deed for drede. Caxton's Divers Fruyt/iil Ghostly Maters.\n\n(1) a pig. A pestle of pork is still in common use. \" Pestels of venison,\" Warner's Antiq. Culin. p. 98. *• Pestell of flesshe,^'amJon,\" Palsgrave. A pestle-pie is a large standing pie which contains a whole gammon, and sometimes a couple of fowls and a neat's tongue, a favorite dish at country fairs, and at Christmas feasts. A constable's staff.\n\nPERTURBE. To trouble. Palsgrave. PERTY. Pait. Lydgate.\n\nPERTY.\n\nPart. Lydgate. God that sittls 1n Trlnité,\n\nGyffe thaym grace wel to the, That lystyns me whyle ;\n\nAlle that lovys of melody,\n\nOffhevon bllsse God graunte tham perty,\n\nTheyrr soules shelde fro peryle:\n\n(2) A constable's staff.\n\nMS:\n\nPÉSTLE-HEAD. blockhead\n\n# PERUR A kind of cup.\n\nPETE. See Cov. Myst.\n\nPERUSE. To examine, or survey.\n\nLong lay the kyng,there away wolde not hee Dayly he propherld batayle : the enmys durst not fyghte Lacke of logynge and vltayle It was grett peté,\n\nthe fleet, returned to the king, and told him there waa nothing ready ; and that the mariners and souldiers would not yeeld to goe the voyage till they were paid their arrears. MS. HarU 383.\n\nCausid the gentill prynce to remeve, siche was Goddes myzte\n\nLowe, how the good Lorde hls owne gentill kuyzle,\n\nPERVEY To provide. (A-N)\n\nBecause he shulde rememblr hym In wele and In woo, Thus in every thyng, Lorde, thy wIlle be doo Reg\n\nPERVINKE. The herb periwinkle. (A-s)\n\nPERYE.\n\npear-tree. (A.-N) But for hur lorde sche durste not done, That sate benethe and pleyed hym merye,\n\nPETEOSE. Merciful compassionate.\n\nMany\n\nspekes of lamentacioun,\n\nmen\n\nBeforc the towre undur a peryo.\n\nOff moders and of thelr gret dcsolatioun,\n\nWhich that thay did indure When that thelr chílder dy and passe, But of hls peteose tender moder, alasre am verray sure,\n\n# PERYSSE. Pears. (A.-N)\n\nAnd began down to falle. MS.\n\nThe wo and payn passis alle othere.\n\nA gorget of mail or plate attached to the helmet. A pesane and a paunsone; MS. Morte Arthure, f. 89.\n\n",
